#!/bin/sh
# Said here as well as in the control file's Description, because a package
# manager is where a false promise costs most: someone installs, launches,
# gets a message they did not expect, and files a bug against a maintainer
# rather than reading a README. The same note ships in
# packaging/aur/stacked-git/stacked-git.install for the same reason.
set -e

case "$1" in
    configure)
        cat <<'MSG'

Stacked ships no Roblox build. `stacked install` fetches one, and installs
it only if Roblox's own signing certificate signed it.

Roblox publishes no Android binary of its own -- its endpoint answers
supportsAndroidBinaries: false -- so the build comes from a third-party
mirror, or from a copy Sober has already unpacked, or from an APK you point
it at:

    ~/.var/app/org.vinegarhq.Sober/data/sober/packages/x86_64/com.roblox.client/

A local copy is preferred when there is one: it costs no request and no
bytes, and it gets the same signature check either way.

Start it by typing `stacked`, or from your application list; `stacked help`
lists the other commands. `cordial-run` is the loader it launches and is not
what you run by hand.

https://github.com/DamnShabu/stacked#readme says which claims were measured
and how.

MSG
        ;;
esac

exit 0
